    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogramma_diplotaenia_en/",
      "html": "<p>This dwarf cichlid is one of the most unusual members of the large genus Apistogramma. The very slim animals have a pattern of two longitudinal stripes. This is unique among Apistogramma and makes A. diplotaenia unmistakable.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogramma_diplotaenia_en/",
      "html": "<p>Sadly this species is offered only very rarely. Both wild-collected and bred fish are currently available.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/apistogramma_diplotaenia_en/",
      "html": "<p>Wild-caught males almost lose the double stripes in dominant or aggressive mood and get an orange-yellow band instead. All in all, in Apistogramma diplotaenia the females have the trousers on and are the more attractive fish, too.</p>"
    }
